2651 0 obj
<</Title(Bad lines)/Dest[18 0 R/Fit]/Parent 2632 0 R/Next 2669 0 R>>
endobj

2652 0 obj
<</Title(Both styles do a constant amount of work perline, so each is O\(1\) per line and O\(n\) for n lines; only the constant differs. When bad lines are rare, EAFP and LBYL are equal and EAFP gives shorter code and avoids race conditions. When bad lines are common \(say more than about 20-30%\), the exception cost makes LBYL roughly 2-3 times faster. For attendance files where damaged lines are rare, the exception design is appropriate; using both \(cheap check for the common faults, exception for the rest\) is also reasonable. Space is O\(1\) extra in both.)/Dest[18 0 R/Fit]/Parent 2632 0 R/Prev 2670 0 R/First 2671 0 R/Last 2672 0 R/Count -2>>
endobj

